  "worked_solution": "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=-\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14242844/assets/1cc1a1f5fa2bf98d700bf0c8d8e0630a4fda0540b8715865ee72d34b17049b8a.png)\n\nSince we have $y=-\\frac{1}{x}\\,-\\,2,$ we need to translate the graph of $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$ *down* by $2$ units.\n\nNow, since the graph of $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$ is translated down by $2$ units, the horizontal asymptote $y=0$ is also translated down by $2$ units. So, the equation of the new horizontal asymptote is $y=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct graph is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14242844/assets/10c52c6f400ff5880358c6653f2cd0341aed312317d88438a545b2522e19b61b.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
